\chapter{Personalities and System Call API}
\label{ch:personality}

\section{Dynamic Personality Redirection}

To support foreign operating system Binaries (such as Linux ELF or
Windows PE executables), Telix incorporates dynamic system call
redirection tables \footcite{gerb01}. Foreign OS ABIs run atop
user-level microkernel applications \footcite{golu91} backed by
distributed \Mach-style cluster messaging
\footcite{zajc93, rash87, milo93}.

\section{Native Completion-Based System Call API}

To eliminate traditional trap-based system call overhead in the case of
high-bandwidth system call issuance, Telix implements a memory-mapped
submission and completion queue interface. This design builds upon the
exception-less system call model introduced by FlexSC\footcite{soar10}
and modern lock-free completion rings such as Linux \iouring
\footcite{axbo19}, where significant inspiration is also taken from
\texttt{k42}\footcite{krie06}.
\\
\Mach port -like facilities are also intended to be retained in parallel
with the memory-mapped submission and completion interface for their
potential to be made cluster-capable, network transparent and
potentially stackable~\footcite{rash87}.

\subsection{Memory-Mapped Submission and Completion Queues}
% \subsection{\texttt{io\_uring}-alike Facility}

The native ABI exposes paired, lock-free ring buffers mapped into both user
space and kernel memory. System call requests are posted to the Submission
Queue (SQ) without triggering a context switch; execution completion entries
are asynchronously posted back to the Completion Queue (CQ).

\subsection{Asynchronous Page Faults and Specialized Upcalls}
\label{sec:async_page_faults}

Rather than using the fully general, heavyweight upcall machinery originally
proposed in classic Scheduler Activations \footcite{ande92}, Telix adopts a
streamlined approach inspired by Nemesis \footcite{lesl96} and K42
\footcite{krie06}. Upcalls are restricted to targeted event channels for
critical non-blocking hotpaths—specifically asynchronous page faults and yield
preemption signals—allowing thread schedulers to react instantly without
rebuilding general stack continuations.

\section{Linux Personality}
\label{sec:linux_eco}

The Linux personality is the most economically significant of the
personality servers: Linux dominates both the embedded and server
markets, and a verified kernel that runs unmodified Linux ELF binaries
is a drop-in replacement for the largest deployed ecosystem in
computing.  The personality server translates POSIX system calls
(\S\ref{sec:linux_personality}) into native Telix capability
operations, with memory-management calls routed through the external
pager (\S\ref{sec:fs_vm_decoupling}).

The technical challenge is not ABI translation — the Linux system-call
interface is well-documented — but matching the performance and
semantic expectations of software written for a monolithic kernel.
Two areas require care: \texttt{epoll} and \texttt{io\_uring}
semantics, which map naturally to Telix's continuation-polling model,
and \texttt{fork}/\texttt{COW}, which must be handled by the external
pager rather than in-kernel virtual-memory code.

\section{Windows Personality}
\label{sec:windows_eco}

The Windows NT personality server (\S\ref{sec:windows_personality})
runs unmodified PE binaries by mapping the Windows Native API
(\texttt{Nt*}/\texttt{Zw*} routines) onto Telix capabilities.
In practice, this personality subsumes the role of WINE: rather than
running WINE as a userspace translation layer atop Linux, the Windows
personality server runs directly on Telix's native capability
transport, eliminating a layer of indirection.  The economic
motivation is the Western desktop market, where Windows binary
compatibility is a prerequisite for adoption.

\section{\OpenHarmony\ Personality}
\label{sec:openharmony_eco}

\OpenHarmony\ is both an economic target (the dominant operating system
for the Chinese IoT and consumer-electronics ecosystem) and a technical
peer: its distributed soft bus, component framework, and Binder-based
IPC are architecturally closer to Telix's capability model than to
Linux's file-descriptor interface.  The personality server emulates
Binder IPC using lock-free shared-memory rings
(\S\ref{sec:nextgen_abis}).

The principal question is whether \OpenHarmony's distributed-device
features can interoperate at a peer level with Telix's clustering
layer, or whether interoperability is limited by policy decisions in
the \OpenHarmony\ component framework rather than by mechanism.  If
the limitation is mechanistic, Telix's transport layer can bridge it;
if it is policy-driven (e.g., device attestation, vendor lock-in), the
personality server provides ABI compatibility but not ecosystem
integration.

\section{Zircon Personality}
\label{sec:zircon_eco}

The Zircon personality targets the Google/Fuchsia ecosystem.  Zircon's
handle-and-channel model is structurally isomorphic to Telix's
capability model, giving near 1:1 syscall translation efficiency
(\S\ref{sec:nextgen_abis}).  The economic question is deployment
breadth: Fuchsia is not yet widely deployed outside Google's own
devices, limiting the immediate practical value of this personality.
It is included for completeness and because the translation cost is
negligible — the isomorphism means the personality server is thin.

