\section{Foreign ABI Emulation and Subsystem Personalities}
\label{sec:foreign_abi_emulation}

While native Telix applications interact directly with capability tokens
and async continuation rings, supporting legacy and component ecosystem
binaries requires mapping foreign Application Binary Interfaces (ABIs)
onto native primitives.

Rather than embedding foreign OS syscall handlers inside the
Framekernel, Telix delegates ABI emulation to isolated user-space
\textit{Personality Servers} \footcite{haertig97l4}.

\subsection{Trap Redirection and User-Space Interception}

When an unmodified foreign binary executes a hardware system call trap
(e.g., x86 \texttt{syscall}, ARM \texttt{svc}, or RISC-V
\texttt{ecall}), execution is handled via one of two paths:

\begin{description}
    \item[\textbf{Fast-Path vDSO / Libc Shim:}] For dynamically linked workloads, Telix supplies a specialized user-space shim library (\texttt{vDSO} or custom \texttt{libc}) that intercepts system call invocations before triggering a hardware trap, encoding the request directly into a native capability ring submission.

    \item[\textbf{OutOf-Domain Trap Redirection:}] Statically linked binaries trigger an architectural fault upon executing a hardware trap. The Framekernel catches the exception and bounces control directly to the registered Personality Server's event loop without executing high-privilege kernel intervention code.
\end{description}

\subsection{Linux POSIX Personality Server}
\label{sec:linux_personality}

The Linux personality server provides POSIX compatibility by wrapping
monolithic Linux abstractions around Telix capabilities
\footcite{daSilva20wsl}:

Memory-management system calls (\texttt{mmap}, COW faults, \texttt{fork})
are not implemented inside the personality server.  They are translated
into external-pager requests to the designated pager server
(\S\ref{sec:fs_vm_decoupling}), so the Linux personality inherits the
same zero-copy page-caching and distributed-paging model as native
processes.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{File Descriptor Table Mapping:} Linux integer file descriptors (`0, 1, 2, ...`) are maintained in a thread-local, lock-free array that translates indices directly into native Telix Capability Tokens.
    \item \textbf{Asynchronous Event Unification:} Linux \texttt{epoll} and \texttt{io\_uring} calls are natively mapped 1:1 to Telix asynchronous continuation polling loops (as detailed in Section~\ref{sec:mn_threading}), eliminating impedance mismatch between Linux event loops and Telix execution state.
    \item \textbf{Signal Serialization:} POSIX signals are converted into serialized event messages pushed to the target task's capability queue, executing signal handlers as high-priority Rust continuations.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Windows NT Subsystem Emulation}
\label{sec:windows_personality}

Unmodified Portable Executable (PE) binaries expecting the Windows
Native API (\texttt{Nt*} / \texttt{Zw*} routines) are served by the NT
personality server \footcite{wine_design}.

Opaque Windows \texttt{HANDLE} references are mapped directly into Telix
capability slots:

